 =====================================
 
 a) Concrete behavior and trace
-   - The original task moves a solid n×n square to another solid n×n square through a
-     river of full n×n windows. Here the moving body is an oriented L polyomino inside
-     an n×n bbox, with n in {3,4}. The source component has one extra same-colored tip
-     pixel attached on one occupied side of the L. Removing that tip yields the real
-     moving body.
-   - The tip is not decorative. It gives the unique initial motion direction and is also
-     reused as the docking orientation cue: the red target is a dock frame whose mouth is
-     on the same side named by the tip direction, and whose blue cavity is the same L
-     silhouette as the mover body.
+   - Original task: a solid n x n square moves through blue space, and destination is another
+     solid n x n square. Reachability depends on whether every cell of the translated square
+     stays inside the blue corridor.
+   - Fused variant:
+     1. The mover body is an L polyomino inside an n x n bbox, with n in {2,3,4}.
+     2. One extra same-colored tip pixel sticks out on one side of that L body. The tip is
+        input-only and indicates the mover's initial outgoing direction.
+     3. The destination is a red docking frame whose inner cavity is an L-shaped blue notch
+        matching the mover body orientation. The frame touches one grid edge and has a mouth
+        on the inward side so the mover can dock into the cavity.
+     4. Motion uses the exact L footprint, not the full n x n bbox. If the dock anchor is
+        reachable, the mover body slides there and nests into the cavity; otherwise it stops
+        at the farthest reachable placement. The tip disappears in the output.
    - Small trace:
-       original:
-         detect two solid squares -> use color order -> test full n×n windows
-         -> if destination window reachable, cover red square.
-       fused:
-         detect the unique "L + tip" component -> remove tip -> get oriented L body
-         -> detect the unique dock frame whose inner blue cavity matches that same L and
-            whose mouth side equals the tip side
-         -> BFS on translated L placements, checking only occupied L cells
-         -> if dock anchor reachable, place the L into the cavity and leave the red frame.
-   - The differences are coupled, not independent:
-       1. The body shape changes the passability test.
-       2. The tip is required to identify the mover and fixes the dock mouth side.
