 =====================================
 
 a) Concrete behavior and trace
-   - The original task moves a solid n×n square to another solid n×n square through a
-     river of full n×n windows. Here the moving body is an oriented L polyomino inside
-     an n×n bbox, with n in {3,4}. The source component has one extra same-colored tip
-     pixel attached on one occupied side of the L. Removing that tip yields the real
-     moving body.
-   - The tip is not decorative. It gives the unique initial motion direction and is also
-     reused as the docking orientation cue: the red target is a dock frame whose mouth is
-     on the same side named by the tip direction, and whose blue cavity is the same L
-     silhouette as the mover body.
+   - Original task: a solid n x n square moves through blue space, and destination is another
+     solid n x n square. Reachability depends on whether every cell of the translated square
+     stays inside the blue corridor.
+   - Fused variant:
+     1. The mover body is an L polyomino inside an n x n bbox, with n in {2,3,4}.
+     2. One extra same-colored tip pixel sticks out on one side of that L body. The tip is
+        input-only and indicates the mover's initial outgoing direction.
+     3. The destination is a red docking frame whose inner cavity is an L-shaped blue notch
+        matching the mover body orientation. The frame touches one grid edge and has a mouth
+        on the inward side so the mover can dock into the cavity.
+     4. Motion uses the exact L footprint, not the full n x n bbox. If the dock anchor is
+        reachable, the mover body slides there and nests into the cavity; otherwise it stops
+        at the farthest reachable placement. The tip disappears in the output.
    - Small trace:
-       original:
-         detect two solid squares -> use color order -> test full n×n windows
-         -> if destination window reachable, cover red square.
-       fused:
-         detect the unique "L + tip" component -> remove tip -> get oriented L body
-         -> detect the unique dock frame whose inner blue cavity matches that same L and
-            whose mouth side equals the tip side
-         -> BFS on translated L placements, checking only occupied L cells
-         -> if dock anchor reachable, place the L into the cavity and leave the red frame.
-   - The differences are coupled, not independent:
-       1. The body shape changes the passability test.
-       2. The tip is required to identify the mover and fixes the dock mouth side.
-       3. The destination is no longer a cover target; it is a shape-matched cavity.
+     * Suppose n=3 and the body is the canonical bottom-left L:
+       {(0,0),(1,0),(2,0),(2,1),(2,2)}.
+     * Original logic would test a full 3 x 3 window. The fused logic tests only those
+       5 occupied cells, so an apparent 3-wide blue strip can still fail if the upper arm
+       cell needed by the L is missing.
+     * If the tip points right, the start placement must have exactly one feasible neighbor
+       to the right. The destination frame might touch the top edge, with a bottom-side mouth
+       leading into a bottom-left L cavity. The mover can only dock if both the corridor is
+       shape-passable and the cavity orientation matches the mover body.
 
 b) Complexity calibration
-   - Only one body family is used: L. This keeps the fused rule readable.
-   - n is restricted to 3 or 4. That makes the L, the cavity, and the tip visually clear
-     and avoids ambiguous 2-cell-wide edge markers.
-   - Tip direction is restricted to one of the L's occupied sides. This reduces latent
-     categories and guarantees that the dock mouth opens onto actual cavity cells.
+   - Only one body family is used: L shapes. This keeps the category count low while still
+     making the geometry visibly different from the original square task.
+   - The only marker is a single protrusion pixel. It is visually salient but does not create
+     additional moving geometry in the output.
+   - Docks always use the same rigid construction: an (n+2) x (n+2) red frame/support with an
+     internal blue L notch. This is large enough to be obvious and restrictive enough that the
+     docking condition is readable across a few examples.
 
-c) Implicit assumptions from the original solver that break
-   - "Mover and target are solid squares" is false. The mover is an L+tip component and
-     the target is a dock frame with an L-shaped hole.
-   - "Lower color is the mover" is false. Object colors are randomized, so role detection
-     must come from shape.
-   - "Feasible if an n×n window is fully traversable" is false. Only the translated L
-     cells matter; background may remain inside the bbox.
-   - "Destination color is traversable" is false. Dock frame pixels are walls; only the
-     blue cavity and mouth are passable.
-   - "Erase / paint whole n×n rectangles" is false. Only the body cells move; the tip is
-     erased at the source and never reappears at the destination.
+c) Hidden assumptions from the original main() that break
+   - "Both key objects are solid squares" is false. The mover is an L plus a tip, and the
+     destination is a structured frame.
+   - "Sort by color to decide mover vs destination" is invalid. The generator randomizes object
+     colors, so the solver must detect the arrow-marked L component and the dock structurally.
+   - "Full n x n window must be traversable" is invalid. Only the occupied L cells matter.
+   - "Destination color is passable" is invalid. Red dock pixels are walls; only river and the
+     mover component itself are traversable during the search.
+   - "Erase / paint entire bbox" is invalid. The output must erase the full marked mover
+     component (body plus tip) but only paint the L body at the terminal position.
 
-d) Placement legality
-   - The anchor path is constructed in bbox top-left coordinates, so every translated L
-     stays in bounds.
-   - The dock anchor is placed one cell in from an outer edge. That leaves room for the
-     outer frame border and keeps the cavity inside the grid.
-   - The dock mask is painted only after the blue path exists; the cavity and mouth are
-     explicitly restored to river color so the solver's passability model matches the
-     intended docking geometry.
-   - Chokepoints remove a rotated subset of one L arm along a straight run. The river
-     remains pixel-connected, but later L placements become infeasible.
+d) Placement legality enforced by the generator
+   - The river is generated from translated copies of the exact L body, so every intended step
+     is in-bounds by construction.
+   - The start placement is accepted only when it has exactly one feasible neighboring placement,
+     making the tip direction unambiguous.
+   - The dock frame is built from a verified mask at an anchor one cell in from a grid edge.
+     Its cavity orientation always matches the mover body.
+   - Stuck cases carve shape-specific chokepoints by deleting one arm pattern from consecutive
+     placements on a straight run. The river stays pixel-connected, but the full L placement
+     becomes impossible beyond the gate.
 
 e) Visual observability
-   - Across examples, the same three visible facts recur:
-       1. the moving object is the only component with a one-pixel arrow tip,
-       2. the path width must fit the full L body,
-       3. the destination is a larger red frame with an L-shaped blue notch.
-   - A human can infer from a few examples that the arrow-marked L slides through blue
-     space and tries to dock into the matching notch, stopping early when the corridor
-     ceases to support the whole L.
+   - Humans can see three simultaneous cues: the mover is the irregular object with a tip, the
+     destination is the frame with a matching L notch, and the mover body orientation matches
+     the notch shape. The tip shows the initial slide direction without relying on color order.
+   - Reachable outputs visibly end with the black/random-colored L nested inside the red frame.
+     Stuck outputs leave the frame empty and stop the mover earlier on the river.
 
 f) Visual cleanliness
-   - Only four semantic regions are present: background, river, mover color, dock color.
-   - The tip is a single extra pixel on the mover, never overprinted on the dock.
-   - The dock stays a single monochromatic frame component; the cavity remains plain blue,
-     so the final nesting is easy to read.
+   - Only four semantic regions remain: background, river, mover color, dock color.
+   - The tip is a single pixel on existing river, so it does not clutter the scene.
+   - The dock is a single connected red component with a clean blue cavity, rather than several
+     separate markers.
 
-g) Color inferability
-   - No hidden color ordering is used. The mover is recognized by "L plus tip", the dock
-     by "frame with matching L cavity". Colors only separate regions.
-
-h) Quantifiable non-degeneration checks used after implementation
-   - Shape-vs-rectangle check: compare the fused solver's target position with a weakened
-     rectangle oracle that uses the mover bbox instead of the L occupancy mask.
-   - Color-order check: measure how often mover color > dock color, proving that old
-     color sorting would fail.
-   - Dock check: verify that reachable samples end inside a detected dock cavity rather
-     than replacing a solid target.
+g) Fusion-behavior audit targets
+   - Rectangle-baseline disagreement: many generated instances should have a different terminal
+     position if one incorrectly uses a full n x n square instead of the L body.
+   - Color-order disagreement: because mover/dock colors are randomized, "smaller color moves"
+     should fail regularly. This prevents silent regression to the original identification rule.
 
-i) New solver assumptions introduced here and guaranteed by the generator
-   - Exactly one non-river component is an L body plus one removable tip pixel.
-   - Exactly one other non-river component is a dock frame matching that L and that tip
-     direction.
-   - The tip direction equals the unique feasible outgoing direction at the source.
-   - The dock cavity top-left anchor is the intended terminal placement.
+h) New solver assumptions introduced here
+   - Exactly one non-river component is an L body plus exactly one side tip pixel.
+   - Exactly one other non-river component is a valid dock mask whose cavity matches that L
