-         detect two solid squares -> use color order -> test full n×n windows
-         -> if destination window reachable, cover red square.
-       fused:
-         detect the unique "L + tip" component -> remove tip -> get oriented L body
-         -> detect the unique dock frame whose inner blue cavity matches that same L and
-            whose mouth side equals the tip side
-         -> BFS on translated L placements, checking only occupied L cells
-         -> if dock anchor reachable, place the L into the cavity and leave the red frame.
-   - The differences are coupled, not independent:
-       1. The body shape changes the passability test.
-       2. The tip is required to identify the mover and fixes the dock mouth side.
-       3. The destination is no longer a cover target; it is a shape-matched cavity.
+     * Suppose n=3 and the body is the canonical bottom-left L:
+       {(0,0),(1,0),(2,0),(2,1),(2,2)}.
+     * Original logic would test a full 3 x 3 window. The fused logic tests only those
+       5 occupied cells, so an apparent 3-wide blue strip can still fail if the upper arm
+       cell needed by the L is missing.
+     * If the tip points right, the start placement must have exactly one feasible neighbor
+       to the right. The destination frame might touch the top edge, with a bottom-side mouth
+       leading into a bottom-left L cavity. The mover can only dock if both the corridor is
+       shape-passable and the cavity orientation matches the mover body.
 
 b) Complexity calibration
-   - Only one body family is used: L. This keeps the fused rule readable.
-   - n is restricted to 3 or 4. That makes the L, the cavity, and the tip visually clear
-     and avoids ambiguous 2-cell-wide edge markers.
-   - Tip direction is restricted to one of the L's occupied sides. This reduces latent
-     categories and guarantees that the dock mouth opens onto actual cavity cells.
+   - Only one body family is used: L shapes. This keeps the category count low while still
+     making the geometry visibly different from the original square task.
+   - The only marker is a single protrusion pixel. It is visually salient but does not create
+     additional moving geometry in the output.
+   - Docks always use the same rigid construction: an (n+2) x (n+2) red frame/support with an
+     internal blue L notch. This is large enough to be obvious and restrictive enough that the
+     docking condition is readable across a few examples.
 
-c) Implicit assumptions from the original solver that break
-   - "Mover and target are solid squares" is false. The mover is an L+tip component and
-     the target is a dock frame with an L-shaped hole.
-   - "Lower color is the mover" is false. Object colors are randomized, so role detection
-     must come from shape.
-   - "Feasible if an n×n window is fully traversable" is false. Only the translated L
-     cells matter; background may remain inside the bbox.
-   - "Destination color is traversable" is false. Dock frame pixels are walls; only the
-     blue cavity and mouth are passable.
-   - "Erase / paint whole n×n rectangles" is false. Only the body cells move; the tip is
-     erased at the source and never reappears at the destination.
