-g) Color inferability
-   - No hidden color ordering is used. The mover is recognized by "L plus tip", the dock
-     by "frame with matching L cavity". Colors only separate regions.
+g) Fusion-behavior audit targets
+   - Rectangle-baseline disagreement: many generated instances should have a different terminal
+     position if one incorrectly uses a full n x n square instead of the L body.
+   - Color-order disagreement: because mover/dock colors are randomized, "smaller color moves"
+     should fail regularly. This prevents silent regression to the original identification rule.
 
-h) Quantifiable non-degeneration checks used after implementation
-   - Shape-vs-rectangle check: compare the fused solver's target position with a weakened
-     rectangle oracle that uses the mover bbox instead of the L occupancy mask.
-   - Color-order check: measure how often mover color > dock color, proving that old
-     color sorting would fail.
-   - Dock check: verify that reachable samples end inside a detected dock cavity rather
-     than replacing a solid target.
-
-i) New solver assumptions introduced here and guaranteed by the generator
-   - Exactly one non-river component is an L body plus one removable tip pixel.
-   - Exactly one other non-river component is a dock frame matching that L and that tip
-     direction.
-   - The tip direction equals the unique feasible outgoing direction at the source.
-   - The dock cavity top-left anchor is the intended terminal placement.
+h) New solver assumptions introduced here
+   - Exactly one non-river component is an L body plus exactly one side tip pixel.
